        "title": "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "83647310-4da7-5902-bf91-11172c042805",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)={4}^{x}\\log _{4} (x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n$f^{\\prime}(x)=4^{x} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={4}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\log _{4} (x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({4}^{x})}^{′}\\log _{4} (x)+{4}^{x}{(\\log _{4} (x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({4}^{x}\\ln  (4))(\\log _{4} (x))+({4}^{x})(\\frac{1}{x\\ln  (4)}) \\\\  & ={4}^{x}\\ln  (4)\\log _{4} (x)+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{x\\ln  (4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "8fe66d8e-57cf-5f76-8de2-4445eb88e70c",
            ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
            ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
            ":answer-field/choices": [
              {
                ":answer/id": "ad1a7cdd-ab15-5b2a-b4c2-89e230db1516",
                ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
                ":answer/value": "\\frac{4^{x}}{x \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}"
              }
            ],
